# Complete Step-by-Step Guide: Enable HTTPS on Spring Boot API Gateway

For your architecture, we will configure **HTTPS only on the Spring Cloud API Gateway**. Your backend microservices will continue using HTTP, and both your Flutter Web and Flutter Mobile applications will connect to the Gateway over HTTPS.

I'll assume you are using Windows for certificate preparation and Spring Boot on a Linux server, although the steps can also be used for local testing on Windows.

## 1. Understand the final architecture

```text
       FLUTTER WEB APPLICATION
                  |
                  | HTTPS
                  |
                  v
          TOMCAT WEB SERVER
                  |
                  | HTTPS (if proxying API requests)
                  v
       SPRING CLOUD API GATEWAY
             Port 8443
                  |
         +--------+--------+
         |        |        |
        HTTP     HTTP     HTTP
         |        |        |
         v        v        v
       Auth     Master   Other
      Service  Service  Services
       :8081    :8082    :8083


       FLUTTER MOBILE APPLICATION
                  |
                  | HTTPS
                  v
       SPRING CLOUD API GATEWAY
             Port 8443
                  |
                  | HTTP
                  v
          BACKEND MICROSERVICES
```

The ports are examples; use your actual service ports.

**What we are going to do:**

1. Obtain the existing certificate files.
2. Check whether the certificate is suitable for the API Gateway.
3. Install OpenSSL on Windows.
4. Convert the certificate and private key into a PKCS12 keystore.
5. Transfer the keystore securely to your Linux server.
6. Configure HTTPS in the Gateway's `application.properties`.
7. Keep backend services on HTTP.
8. Configure Flutter to use the HTTPS Gateway URL.
9. Test the complete communication.

